<section class="sec" id="evidence">
  <div class="sec-head">
    <p class="kicker">The problem, in published numbers</p>
    <h2>Foundries lose real money to rejected castings</h2>
    <p class="lead">Peer-reviewed and published case studies report rejection rates from 5% to as high as 45%. Dimensional and sizing faults are a recurring cause, and every rejected casting carries its full material, melting and labour cost.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="stats">
    <div class="stat"><div class="big">10–45%<small> daily</small></div><p>Rejection range found on one automobile transfer-case casting in an Indian foundry. A diagnostic study cut it to 4% from 28%.</p><button type="button" data-rej="28">Use 28% in the model</button><cite>Chokkalingam &amp; Nazirudeen, 2009 [1]</cite></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="big">8.5%<small> → 3.5%</small></div><p>Rejection fell when an Indian foundry replaced trial and error with simulation on a cylinder clamp casting.</p><button type="button" data-rej="8.5">Use 8.5% in the model</button><cite>Reducing Rejection Rate of Castings using Simulation Model [2]</cite></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="big">27%<small> → 9.8%</small></div><p>Six Sigma plus casting simulation lifted material yield to 55.3% on a green-sand product.</p><button type="button" data-rej="27">Use 27% in the model</button><cite>Inderscience, Six Sigma &amp; simulation study [3]</cite></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="big">5–7%</div><p>Annual rejection at a modern, automated German sand foundry. Even best-in-class plants still scrap parts.</p><button type="button" data-rej="6">Use 6% in the model</button><cite>Int. J. Metalcasting, 2023 [4]</cite></div>
  </div>
</section>

